@page "/HowItWorks/30-day-rule"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/30-day-rule">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <p>
                After the <a href="HowItWorks/same-day-rule" class="link-styled">same-day rule</a>, any shares still unmatched
                in a sale are matched with shares of the same company bought in the next 30 days, before the
                <a href="HowItWorks/section-104-pool" class="link-styled">Section 104 pool</a> is used. This is the "bed and
                breakfast" rule. In the app:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Window:</strong> acquisitions from 1 to 30 days after the disposal date, counted in calendar
                    days. Acquisitions on the disposal day itself are handled by the same-day rule first.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Order:</strong> the earliest acquisition in the window is matched first (TCGA 1992 s106A(5)(b)).
                    If several disposals could use the same later purchase, the earlier disposal goes first (s106A(4)).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Non-residents:</strong> an acquisition made on or after 22 March 2006 while you are not UK
                    resident is not matched under this rule (s106A(5A), see
                    <a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg51560" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CG51560</a>).
                    The app uses the periods you set on the <a href="ResidencyStatusPage" class="link-styled">Residency Status</a>
                    page. The rule also covers acquisitions made while you are "Treaty non-resident", but the app has no
                    separate setting for that.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Corporate actions in the window:</strong> if a stock split or similar event falls between the
                    disposal and the repurchase, the matched quantity is adjusted for it.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Only what you import is seen:</strong> purchases inside an ISA or pension should not be in your
                    imported files, so they are never matched. Do include any trades in the 30 days after the tax year ends,
                    because they can change that year's result.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Matches made under this rule are labelled "Bed and breakfast" on the
                <a href="TradeMatchPage" class="link-styled">Trade Matches</a> page.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">Worked example</h2>
                <p>
                    You bought 1,000 shares in May 2024 for £4,990 plus a £10 fee, so the pool holds 1,000 shares costing
                    £5,000. On 3 March 2025 you sell all 1,000 for £4,000 less a £10 fee (net £3,990). On 20 March 2025
                    you buy 1,000 back for £4,100 plus a £10 fee (£4,110).
                </p>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    The sale is matched with the March repurchase, not with the pool: £3,990 − £4,110 = a
                    <strong>loss of £120</strong>. The pool keeps the original 1,000 shares at £5,000.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="https://cgtvisualiser.co.uk/guides/30-day-rule/" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CGT Visualiser: 30-day rule</a> (independent site)</li>
        <li><a href="HowItWorks/residency" class="link-styled">Residency status</a>: how non-resident periods change matching</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg51560" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HMRC CG51560</a>: the same-day and 30-day rules</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1992/12/section/106A" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TCGA 1992 s106A</a></li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
